Wheel Clamp:
  Wheel clamps/ wheel locks are devices that are clamped onto the wheels of cars. They are intended to prevent the car being moved, often due to illegal parking or
  vehicle repossession.
  Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wheel_clamp
  Image source: https://www.yarrasupply.com.au/products/wheel-clamp-13-14-15?srsltid=AfmBOoqbV9Sppl8ziscTUHfojuuwpfVP9_8OR95wT99yxf9CwvXr2NSs
RFID Card Reader:
  Radio Frequency Identification locks use electromagnetic fields to enable communication between two devices, often a card/ fob and a reader. These are often used in
  industry and businesses to provide a secondary layer of security when trying to access a building/ room. The RFID tag communicates with the reader via radio waves.
  The tag must be programmed with a unique credential that will identify and allow access for authorised people. However, there is no guarantee that the person using
  the tag is the same person the tag belongs to.
  Source: https://www.assaabloy.com/au/en/stories/blog/rfid-door-locks--smart-entry-systems
  Image source: cykeorfid.com/rfid-door-lock-access-control-system-the-subtle-power-behind-secure-doors/
Biometric Lock
  Biometric locks use biometric technology to secure doors. It uses things like fingerprints, voice recognition, retina scans or facial recognition. These are all things
  that are inherant to a person, and cannot be easily replicated by someone trying to gain unauthorised access. These locks don't require remembering any passwords, PINs,
  or keys, just physical, biological features.
  Source: https://www.nordencommunication.com/en/blog/what-is-biometric-smart-lock
